## 2026-08-17 — Bootstrap 5.3 CSS-var-only re-theming needs component-level overrides too
Overriding `--bs-primary` and the `-bg-subtle`/`-text-emphasis`/`-rgb` family at `:root` looked complete but wasn't: Bootstrap 5.3's component mixins (`.btn-primary`, form-control focus rings, `.form-check-input:checked`) bake the original `$primary` Sass value into their own local CSS vars at compile time, not `var(--bs-primary)`. Confirmed live in a browser — the login button and every focus ring rendered stock blue despite correct root tokens — not caught by reading the compiled CSS alone. Fixed by overriding each used component's local vars explicitly (see `static/css/theme-overrides.css`'s top comment). Also: Bootstrap's own `.text-bg-*` badge utilities ship with `!important`, so overrides need it too.

## 2026-08-17 — SecurityConfig's CSP blocks all CDN assets by design
CSP is locked to `'self'` everywhere (script/style/font-src) — a deliberate PRD §19.6 decision, not an oversight. A CDN font (Bunny Fonts) was ruled out for exactly this reason. Same-origin webjars are the fix: `org.webjars.npm:<pkg>` on Maven Central mirrors most npm packages, including font packages (`fontsource__inter`) that bundle actual font files with local `@font-face` paths — zero CSP change needed.

## 2026-08-17 — Stale `tenant.primary_color` default caught by grepping the whole repo, not just touched files
Changing the app's theme color in CSS didn't catch two other places the old color lived: an inline-HTML email button (`IdentityEmails.java`) and a DB column default (`tenant.primary_color`, seeded into the first tenant's row). Neither showed up searching templates/CSS. Worth a repo-wide grep for the literal old hex value whenever a "the app's color/theme is X" decision changes, not just the files obviously in scope.

## 2026-09-09 — Adding a second default-active Bootstrap tab pane broke the existing approver flow, caught only by Playwright
Sub-phase 1.9 added a "Tasks" pane to For Action alongside the existing "Time off requests" approvals pane, and made Tasks the default-active pill for everyone. That silently regressed the approver's page: `mvnw verify`'s unit/integration layer never noticed (MockMvc content assertions don't care which Bootstrap tab-pane has `show active`), but `LeaveApprovalE2ETest`'s real headless-browser click on "Approve" timed out — the button existed in the DOM but was inside a `display:none` inactive pane. Fixed by making the default-active pane role-dependent (approvers default to Time off requests, since that's what they came to do; Tasks defaults active only for a plain Employee with no approvals pane at all). Lesson: a second default-active tab/pane sharing a page with an existing one needs to preserve the *existing* users' default, not just add a new option — and CSS-visibility bugs like this are exactly what the Playwright E2E layer catches that MockMvc structurally cannot.

## 2026-10-31 — pg_dump as the app role fails because every tenant table forces row-level security
The first backup run failed with "query would be affected by row-level security policy". The app's own role is subject to FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY, so it cannot dump other tenants' rows; that is the isolation working. Backups run as the `postgres` superuser, which bypasses RLS. Only running the script for real (not reading it) revealed this.

## 2026-10-31 — Spring Security's rules apply on the actuator management port too
Moving the actuator to its own loopback port did not make `/actuator/prometheus` scrapeable: the same filter chain answered 302 to the login page because only `/actuator/health` was permitted. A port-scoped matcher (`SecurityConfig.prometheusOnManagementPort`) permits it on the management port only. Assume every port goes through the application's security chain.

## 2026-10-31 — A generated secret was printed into the session by shell quoting
Writing a SQL command over SSH with `$$`-quoted literals made the remote shell expand `$$` to a process id, the command failed, and the error text echoed the generated database password. It had not been used yet, so it was replaced. Pass SQL through stdin and discard error output whenever a secret is part of the statement.

## 2026-10-31 — Caddy's wildcard certificate stalled on an unreachable IPv6 resolver
DNS-01 issuance kept retrying with "network is unreachable" because the host's resolver list included an IPv6 address the server could not reach, and the propagation check used it. Setting `resolvers 1.1.1.1 1.0.0.1` in the Caddyfile's `tls` block fixed it within one retry.
